WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:06.200
SPEAKER 1: E. Christian
Kopff is a Professor Emeritus

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.930
of Classics at the University
of Colorado Boulder where

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:11.270
he taught for 36 years.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:13.700
He earned his BA at
Haverford College and his PhD

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.150
in Classics from the University
of North Carolina at Chapel

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.170
Hill.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:21.710
From 2004 to 2011, he served
as the Founding Director

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.220
of the Center for
Western Civilization,

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.690
a fellow of the American
Academy in Rome,

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.490
and a recipient of
the Hero of Conscience

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.890
Award from the American
Freedom Alliance.

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:34.400
Dr. Kopff was named Magister
Magnus by the Consortium

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.280
for Classical Lutheran
Education, the organization's

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.330
highest honor.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:42.890
He is the editor of a critical
edition of Euripedes' Bacchae

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:45.200
and author of The
Devil Knows Latin:

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.900
Why America Needs the
Classical Tradition.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.817
Please join me in
welcoming Dr. Kopff.

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:55.687
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.750
E. CHRISTIAN KOPFF: Thank
you for that very nice

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.770
introduction.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.370
Welcome, ladies and gentlemen.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.270
It's an honor to be here.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:16.790
And I'd like to thank Hillsdale
College and the Center

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:20.510
for Constructive
Alternatives for setting up

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.750
this seminar on such
an important topic

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.870
and for allowing
me to participate.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:28.550
And for giving me
my topic, which

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:33.920
is History and the Liberal Arts.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.220
In his dialogue De
re Publica, Cicero

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:41.150
has Scipio explain
how to begin speech.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:44.390
He says, quote, "I
begin by observing

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:48.200
a rule which all
speakers I fancy must

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.780
adhere to if confusion
is to be avoided.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:54.080
That is, if the name of the
subject under discussion

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.640
is accepted, whatever it
is, the meaning of the name

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.200
should be explained."

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.380
End quote.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.010
Well, the title of this talk is
History and the Liberal Arts,

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:07.172
so we can start off by
asking, what is history?

00:02:07.172 --> 00:02:08.630
And we're going
to, in fact, use it

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:13.380
in several senses in ordinary
language and in this talk.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.120
First, it may mean what
happened in the past.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.420
The Battle of Hastings,
the Reign of Henry VIII,

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.180
the good news of
Jesus and how it

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:26.450
spread from Jerusalem to Rome.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.870
Second, history may refer
to studying and recounting

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.060
the past in many forms.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.540
Textbooks, documentaries,
works of literature

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:40.900
like Herodotus's Histories
or Gibbon's Decline and Fall.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.260
Third, as an
academic discipline,

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:46.240
history is one
subject among many--

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.240
social sciences,
arts, humanities,

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.910
and physical sciences--

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.620
that comprise the
undergraduate curriculum

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.170
of a liberal arts education.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:59.740
There were seven liberal
arts that St. Augustine first

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:03.130
cataloged in the
fourth century AD.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:07.450
Three formed the arts of
language, the trivium.

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:12.860
Grammar, dialectic or
logic, and rhetoric.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:17.920
Four comprised the arts of
mathematics, the quadrivium.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:23.080
Arithmetic, geometry,
astronomy, and music.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.350
Why are these arts liberal?

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:30.480
In her award-winning book
Cultivating Humanity:

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.630
A Classical Defense of
Reform in Liberal Education,

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:37.060
Martha Nussbaum writes
of what she calls,

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:42.670
quote, "an education that is
liberal in that it liberates

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:46.720
the mind from the bondage
of habit and custom."

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.610
End quote.

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:50.640
Since she holds
a PhD in classics

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.260
from Harvard University,
Professor Nussbaum

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.210
knows better.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:59.650
The adjective liberal comes from
the Latin word liber, free, not

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:00.835
a slave.

00:04:00.835 --> 00:04:02.680
According to
Aristotle, free people

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:06.040
act after deliberation
and discussion,

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.070
while slaves obey
without debate.

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:10.720
Over the centuries
the liberal arts

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:15.520
have educated students for
many roles in many societies.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.740
Ancient, medieval, and modern.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:21.760
In America, and especially
in the age of the founders,

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:23.950
the freedom of the
liberal arts has

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:28.690
been first and foremost
the freedom of a citizen.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:31.150
Thomas Jefferson,
for instance, wrote

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.630
to his state legislator,
Colonel Charles Yancey,

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:37.780
on January the 6, 1816.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:42.770
Quote, "If a nation expects
to be ignorant and free

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:45.760
in a state of civilization,
it expects what

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:48.740
never was and never will be."

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:49.690
End quote.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:52.870
Jefferson described his
bill for public education

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:56.080
in his only book, Notes
on the State of Virginia,

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.710
final edition 1787.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:04.000
First, students should be taught
to read, write, and reckon.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.520
In addition,
Jefferson said, quote,

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.860
"Their memories
may here be stored

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:14.230
with the most useful facts
from Grecian, Roman, European,

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:15.580
and American history."

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:16.630
End quote.

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:18.550
For Thomas Jefferson,
knowledge of history

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:23.500
was as fundamental and essential
as the grammar and arithmetic

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:25.810
of the seven liberal arts.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:30.070
He explains, quote, "Of all
the views of this law--"

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:32.110
the one on public education--

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:36.310
"none is more important, none
more legitimate than that

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:39.940
of rendering the
people the safe as they

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:44.560
are the ultimate guardians
of their own liberty.

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.020
For this purpose, the
reading in the first stage

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:50.080
when they will receive
their whole education

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:54.550
is proposed as has been said
to be chiefly historical.

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:57.550
History, by appraising
them of the past,

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:00.460
will enable them to
judge of the future.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:04.030
It will avail them of the
experience of other times

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:05.830
and other nations.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:09.430
It will qualify them as
judges of the actions

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.530
and designs of men.

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:14.020
It will enable them
to know ambition

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:16.960
under every disguise
it may assume

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.110
and knowing it to
defeat its views."

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:21.490
End quote.

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:25.150
Every citizen should know
what Jefferson called

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:27.610
the useful facts of history.

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:32.380
But the better educated should
actually read great historians.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:36.070
Jefferson wrote young Peter
Carr on August the 19,

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:39.730
1785, about studying
at William and Mary.

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:41.680
He wrote, quote, "I
advise you to begin

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:44.380
a course of ancient
history, reading everything

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:47.860
in the original and
not translation.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:50.890
Having ascribed proper
hours to exercise,

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:53.740
divide what remains
into three portions.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:58.270
Give the principle portion
to history, the other two,

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:02.050
which should be shorter,
to philosophy and poetry."

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:03.340
End quote.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:05.410
Jefferson urged
reading historians

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:07.180
in the original languages.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:09.930
He commented in the Notes
on the State of Virginia,

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:11.980
quote, "The learning
Greek and Latin

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:15.670
I am told is going
into disuse in Europe.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:19.360
I know not what their manners
and occupations may call for,

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:23.320
but it would be very ill
judged in us to follow them

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:25.030
in this instance."

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:26.590
End quote.

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:29.860
Many American founders
and English Whigs

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:32.350
shared Jefferson's
belief in the importance

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:34.270
of studying ancient history.

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:37.040
One was the English
journalist Thomas Gordon,

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.410
who lived from 1651 to 1750.

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:44.280
Gordon is best known as the
co-author with James Trenchard

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:47.940
of a series of 144
political essays

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.400
espousing the weak
cause in England

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:53.400
and appearing under
the pseudonym of Cato,

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:56.670
the traditionalist
contemporary of Cicero.

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:59.670
They were published in book
form after Trenchard's death

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:03.660
as Cato's Letters, which
was a bestseller in England

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:05.460
and the American colonies.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:09.090
Bernard Bailyn of Harvard in
his study of colonial pamphlets

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:12.210
called Cato's Letters,
quote, "The most frequently

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:16.230
cited authority on matters
of principle and theory."

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:17.250
End quote.

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:20.580
Clinton Rossiter of Cornell
in Seedtime of the Republic

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.670
wrote, quote, "No one
can spend any time

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:27.720
on the newspapers, literary
library inventories,

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:30.000
and pamphlets of
colonial America

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.809
without realizing that Cato's
Letters rather than John

00:08:33.809 --> 00:08:37.320
Locke's Civil Government,
was the most popular,

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:41.549
quotable, esteemed source
for political ideas

00:08:41.549 --> 00:08:43.650
in the colonial period."

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:44.520
End quote.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:46.740
The memory of Cato's
Letters survives

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:48.450
in the name of the
major libertarian

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:52.120
think tank in Washington,
DC, the Cato Institute.

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.090
It's not named after
an ancient Roman.

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:56.760
It's named after the
pseudonymous author

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.560
of Cato's Letters.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.530
After Trenchard's
death in 1724, Gordon

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:04.620
stopped writing fiercely
polemical essays

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:07.170
on contemporary
issues and devoted

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:10.590
the rest of his life to
translating two Roman

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:16.110
historians, Tacitus in
two volumes, 1728 and 1731

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:20.430
with later revisions,
and Sallust in 1744.

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:25.860
He devoted four years, 1720
to 1724, to Cato's Letters,

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:31.830
but 20 years, 1724 to
1744, to ancient history.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:34.920
He prefaced his translations
with lively essays

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:38.100
showing their relevance to the
themes of free institutions,

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:40.680
citizenship, and tyranny.

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:44.910
The pages of Tacitus are full
of tyrannical emperors and brave

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:49.320
senators who stand up to
them and are defeated.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.780
The American founders
saw their world

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:56.640
through the lens of Tacitus
as adjusted by Thomas Gordon.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.700
For John Adams, "The
morality of Tacitus

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:02.550
is the morality of patriotism."

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:06.660
You should know that Whigs
called themselves patriots.

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:10.470
In retirement, Jefferson wrote,
"I read one or two newspapers

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:14.640
a week, but with reluctance
give up even that much time

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:17.310
from Tacitus and Horace."

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:20.160
For John Quincy
Adams in 1819 when

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:22.590
he was Secretary
of State, quote,

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:26.280
"To live without a Cicero
and a Tacitus at hand

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:31.290
seems to me as if it were a
privation of one of my limbs."

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:32.640
End quote.

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:36.270
Modern historians find
it impossible to believe

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:39.660
that English Whigs and American
founders took the past so

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:40.890
seriously.

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:43.620
Stanford's Jack Rakove
writes of the framers

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:47.580
of the US Constitution in his
Pulitzer Prize-winning book

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:50.520
Original Meanings:
Politics and Ideas

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:52.620
in the Making of
the Constitution.

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:55.410
Rakove writes, quote,
"The convention

00:10:55.410 --> 00:11:00.540
delegates of 1787 to '88
came to their task fresh

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:03.000
not from research
in their libraries,

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.790
but from the business
of managing a revolution

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:09.660
and conducting public affairs
at every level of government.

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:13.080
Conscious as they were of
the fate of other republics

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.810
and confederacies,
ancient and modern,

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:17.730
the lessons of the
past they weighed

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:21.420
most heavily were drawn
from their own experience."

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:22.990
End quote.

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:25.480
Is Rakove right?

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:29.710
In mid-August 1787, John Wilson
of Pennsylvania and James

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:33.580
Madison of Virginia tried to
amend the convention's vote

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:36.910
on July the 16 about the
two houses of Congress

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:38.920
by moving that bills
of appropriation

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:42.880
be allowed to originate
in either house, not just

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:45.190
the House of Representatives.

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:47.080
Their forceful
speeches were based

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:51.040
on reason and practical
political observation,

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:54.040
what Rakove calls
"their own experience."

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:58.420
They argued, one, there
was no logical reason

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:01.660
to restrict such bills from
proceeding from either house,

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:05.620
since both houses had to
approve the bills in any event.

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:09.910
In addition, two, the lower
houses of state legislators

00:12:09.910 --> 00:12:15.340
had often behaved irresponsibly
over the decades since 1776.

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:17.680
Their motion was
soundly defeated

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:20.320
and historians
rarely mention it.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:22.090
The debate is
important, however,

00:12:22.090 --> 00:12:24.340
because of the
response it provoked

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:27.490
from distinguished elder
statesman John Dickinson, who

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:29.260
in opposing the
amendment expressed

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:32.980
clearly and explicitly
the principles that guided

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:36.040
many framers.

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:38.650
We have Dickinson's comments,
by the way, because Madison

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:42.400
kept extensive
notes on the debates

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:45.880
in the convention, which
were published decades later.

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:49.210
Dickinson spoke,
quote, "Experience

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:52.030
must be our only guide.

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:55.210
Reason may lead us astray.

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:57.520
It was not reason
that discovered

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:00.970
the singular and admirable
mechanism of the English

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:02.230
Constitution.

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:06.610
It was not reason that
discovered or even could

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:10.240
have discovered the
odd, and in the eyes

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:14.680
of those who were governed
by reason, the absurd mode

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:16.750
of trial by jury.

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:19.780
Accidents probably
produced these discoveries

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:23.170
and experience has
given sanction to them.

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:25.290
This is then our guide.

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:28.300
And has not experience
verified the utility

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:31.720
of restraining money bills to
the immediate representatives

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:32.820
of the people?

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:36.130
Whence the effect may have
proceeded he could not say,

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:40.240
but the effect was visible
and could not be doubted.

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:43.660
Shall we oppose
this long experience

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:48.400
to the short experience of 11
years, which we had ourselves

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:49.390
on this subject?"

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:50.800
End quote.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:54.250
Dickinson's rhetorical question
refutes Jack Rakove's dictum

00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:56.170
which was, as you
remember, "the lessons

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:59.140
of the past that the
framers weighed most heavily

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:01.280
was drawn from their
own experience."

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:03.550
In fact, almost the
opposite's true.

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:06.940
What weighed most heavily with
the delegates in Philadelphia

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:10.300
during the summer
of 1787 was not

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:14.740
what Dickinson called "the short
experience of 11 years, which

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:19.110
we had ourselves," which
is what we today mean when

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:22.090
we use the word experience.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:22.690
No.

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:26.720
What mattered most to them was
English history and common law,

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:28.600
which was based on precedent.

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:31.720
For John Dickinson and the
delegates who voted with him,

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:35.440
experience meant primarily
history and tradition.

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:38.350
As historian Douglas
Adair noted, quote,

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:42.280
"Experience shows our history
proves our expressions that

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:45.640
are used interchangeably
throughout the convention

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:49.120
by members from all sections
of the United States."

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:50.490
End quote.

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:54.070
ME Bradford entitled his
book on the founders A Better

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:56.050
Guide Than Reason.

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:58.990
But that's not quite right.

00:14:58.990 --> 00:15:02.410
Dickinson did not say
that history and tradition

00:15:02.410 --> 00:15:04.570
were better than rationalism.

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:10.540
He said "Experience
must be our only guide."

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:13.660
The history of the Philadelphia
convention and American history

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:17.830
in general needs to be
rewritten to show Americans time

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:22.030
and again privileging,
biblical, classical, German,

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:23.830
and English history
and tradition

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:26.230
over Enlightenment rationalism.

00:15:26.230 --> 00:15:29.100
Recent American history
needs to be analyzed

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:32.440
in light of the catastrophic
consequences of privileging

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:37.960
an abstract rationalism
over history and tradition.

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:39.730
The founders thought
the study of history

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:42.010
essential for education.

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:44.080
History as an
academic discipline

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:47.930
has seen the steepest decline
in measures of all disciplines

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:50.920
since the 2008
recession, according

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:53.800
to the American Historical
Association's journal

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:56.030
Perspectives on History.

00:15:56.030 --> 00:15:57.820
Why has the percentage
of undergraduates

00:15:57.820 --> 00:16:02.260
who major in history shrunk
so much in recent decades?

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:04.690
Often, students
prefer STEM majors

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:07.870
to humanities and
social and history

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:13.540
because they expect STEM to
leave them to good paying jobs.

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:16.900
These expectations
may be exaggerated.

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:19.660
A professor in one prestigious
biology department told

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:21.940
a group of colleagues,
"Our majors

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:25.810
think they're all going to
med school, but only about 20%

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:28.150
of them get accepted."

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:30.370
According to a report
from the US Census

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:37.450
Bureau published on July
the 10, 2014, quote, "74%--

00:16:37.450 --> 00:16:42.440
74% of those who have a
bachelor's degree in Science,

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:45.190
Technology,
Engineering, and Math,

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:48.580
commonly referred
to as STEM, are not

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:51.400
employed in STEM occupations."

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:52.420
End quote.

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:54.160
74%.

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:58.570
STEM grads often find that
although their first job does

00:16:58.570 --> 00:17:01.090
pay more than those of
liberal arts students,

00:17:01.090 --> 00:17:03.580
they eventually hit
a glass ceiling.

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:07.329
During the Republican
primary debates in 2016,

00:17:07.329 --> 00:17:09.940
Senator Rubio of Florida
opined that America

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:13.930
needed, quote, "More welders
and less philosophers."

00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:15.730
End quote.

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:18.940
He meant fewer philosophers, but
let's not get distracted here.

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:19.990
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:19.990 --> 00:17:22.780
Don't get me
started on that one.

00:17:22.780 --> 00:17:25.960
On the stage debating
him was a Stanford

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:29.980
graduate with a history
major and a philosophy minor,

00:17:29.980 --> 00:17:32.530
two subjects often
pointed out as ones

00:17:32.530 --> 00:17:35.950
that don't lead to
high-paying first jobs.

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:38.800
Her first job indeed
was as a secretary,

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:40.660
confirming the prejudice.

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:45.070
Her last job was CEO
of Hewlett-Packard.

00:17:45.070 --> 00:17:46.120
OK.

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:49.990
Carly Fiorina's is an
extreme case, I grant you,

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:52.180
but not unique either.

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:54.910
University of Colorado
President Bruce Benson

00:17:54.910 --> 00:17:57.070
wrote the faculty
in August the 4,

00:17:57.070 --> 00:18:00.010
2016, that his
administration had, quote,

00:18:00.010 --> 00:18:05.590
"surveyed the degrees earned by
the CEOs of Colorado's top 25

00:18:05.590 --> 00:18:06.640
companies.

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:09.970
We found that most had
an undergraduate degree

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:13.930
in the liberal arts and
only then an advanced degree

00:18:13.930 --> 00:18:17.650
in specialized areas
such as law or business."

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:19.450
End quote.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:20.860
There is a deeper
reason, though,

00:18:20.860 --> 00:18:22.960
for the marginalization
of history

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:25.810
and the arts of language
in favor of STEM

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:27.820
and the arts of mathematics.

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:30.700
For most of Western history,
the seven liberal arts

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:35.110
have proclaimed the equal value
of language and mathematics,

00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:37.060
trivium and quadrivium.

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:39.460
But usually, the
arts of language

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:42.520
have been more influential
and widely studied.

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:44.680
The growing prestige
of Plato's vision

00:18:44.680 --> 00:18:47.770
of science as best
done by mathematics

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:51.940
has privileged disciplines
that use mathematical models.

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:54.490
In my experience at the
University of Colorado's Honors

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:57.910
program, honors theses
based on mathematical models

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:02.050
are viewed as more impressive
than those arguing from history

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:04.000
and political philosophy.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:07.690
Aristotle reminded his students
that mathematical models

00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:10.060
favored by his
teacher, Plato, need

00:19:10.060 --> 00:19:13.060
to be confirmed by observation.

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:17.690
Sometimes history of mathematics
suggest different results.

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:19.600
So let me give an example.

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:22.810
Mathematical models suggest
that global free trade

00:19:22.810 --> 00:19:26.410
is superior to the products of
national economies protected

00:19:26.410 --> 00:19:30.550
by the tariff because goods
are cheaper for consumers.

00:19:30.550 --> 00:19:33.220
Economic history,
however, suggests

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:35.380
a different conclusion.

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:37.510
At the time of the American
founding, the United

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:41.780
States where what we today would
call a third world economy,

00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:45.790
one largely agricultural
and producing raw materials

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:47.500
for British factories.

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:50.920
The federal government was
funded by tariffs and excise

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:55.180
taxes, which taxed foreign
countries and businesses.

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:57.400
Many Americans wanted
the tariff used only

00:19:57.400 --> 00:20:00.460
to meet the government's
budgetary needs,

00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:06.220
but others like Alexander
Hamilton and later Henry Clay

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:09.940
wanted a protective tariff
to favor American industry

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:12.040
over foreign competition.

00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:15.160
When the Republican Party
achieved a majority in Congress

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:19.210
after the secession of the
southern states in 1861,

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:23.140
they passed the
protectionist moral tariff.

00:20:23.140 --> 00:20:26.500
President James Buchanan
signed it into law his last day

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:27.730
in office.

00:20:27.730 --> 00:20:31.810
Abraham Lincoln later
signed two stronger versions

00:20:31.810 --> 00:20:33.565
of the tariff.

00:20:33.565 --> 00:20:37.050
As Alfred E. Eckes showed in
his book, Opening America's

00:20:37.050 --> 00:20:39.660
Markets, in the century
after the founding,

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:43.740
the United States developed
from a producer of raw materials

00:20:43.740 --> 00:20:46.710
to an industrial
powerhouse under a regime

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:50.250
of national autarky
funded by the tariff.

00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:53.370
The 16th Amendment gave
free trader Woodrow Wilson

00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:56.550
a chance to sabotage the
tariff, but this was reversed

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:58.530
by Harding and Coolidge.

00:20:58.530 --> 00:21:01.140
Free traders attribute the
prosperity of the roaring

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:03.780
'20s to lower income tax rates.

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:06.990
But Coolidge himself in his
essay on William McKinley

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:09.960
attributes it to the tariff.

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:12.870
In this essay, Coolidge
praises McKinley because he,

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:16.780
quote, "Took up the work
of Hamilton and Clay,

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:20.830
reestablished their principles,
and under his leadership,

00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:23.610
the government re-adopted
their policies."

00:21:23.610 --> 00:21:25.080
End quote.

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:27.700
Their principles, as
the essay makes plain,

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:29.040
were protectionism.

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:32.220
And their policies,
the protective tariff.

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:34.170
FDR worked to
undermine the tariff,

00:21:34.170 --> 00:21:37.320
but the demolition of
America's industrial base

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:39.360
and the hollowing out
of our middle class

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:41.970
really took off with the
Kennedy round of GATT

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:47.070
in the late 1960s followed
by NAFTA in the 1990s.

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:50.040
Of course, the
roaring '20s ended

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:51.930
with the Great Depression.

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:54.540
Marxists blamed it on the
inherent contradictions

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:58.920
of capitalism while free traders
blamed the Smoot-Hawley tariff,

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:01.770
which did not go into effect
until the Depression had

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:03.750
already begun.

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:06.570
Milton Friedman and
Anna J. Schwartz

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:11.340
in A Monetary History of the
United States published in 1963

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:12.840
showed that the
Great Depression was

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:16.080
brought on by the monetary
policies of the Federal

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:17.490
Reserve.

00:22:17.490 --> 00:22:20.250
It was for this work
of economic history,

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:22.660
not his advocacy of
global free trade,

00:22:22.660 --> 00:22:26.280
that Milton Friedman was awarded
the Nobel Prize for Economics

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:28.260
in 1976.

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:31.200
If we're to make rational
decisions about our nation's

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:33.990
future, economic
and otherwise, we

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:36.720
need to supplement the
lessons of mathematics

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:39.810
with those of history.

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:43.380
Classical educators immerse
their students in the past

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:46.320
by studying dead
languages and history.

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:48.540
Progressive educators
think the future will

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:52.500
belong to those who innovate,
and so our education

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:54.750
must be innovative.

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:56.490
Support for the
classical model comes

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:59.040
from the research of
psychologist K. Anders

00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:01.220
Ericsson of Florida State.

00:23:01.220 --> 00:23:04.500
Ericsson studies the most
successful practitioners

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:08.100
of a wide range of different
skills and professions

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:13.440
from policing to chess,
whom he calls experts.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:17.790
Ericsson is the
expert on experts.

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:20.460
Let's examine his
research on chess.

00:23:20.460 --> 00:23:23.160
Chess is a challenging
intellectual activity.

00:23:23.160 --> 00:23:26.130
The communis opinio
is that grandmasters

00:23:26.130 --> 00:23:30.000
win by thinking ahead more
moves than their opponents.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:33.630
Ericsson's research, however,
shows a significant correlation

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:35.820
not with innovation,
but with hours

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:40.680
spent studying the games
of earlier grandmasters.

00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:43.890
Joshua Foer discusses
this in his book,

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:45.720
Moonwalking with Einstein.

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:46.770
Let me quote Foer.

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:50.670
Quote, "Using
magnetoencephalography,

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:54.690
a technique that measures the
weak magnetic fields given off

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:57.120
by a thinking brain,
researchers have

00:23:57.120 --> 00:24:00.150
found that higher
rated chess players are

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:04.230
more likely to engage the
frontal and parietal cortices

00:24:04.230 --> 00:24:06.960
of the brain when they
look at the board, which

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:09.900
suggests they are
recalling information

00:24:09.900 --> 00:24:11.850
from long-term memory.

00:24:11.850 --> 00:24:14.820
Lower ranked players are
more likely to engage

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:18.270
the medial temporal
lobes, which suggests they

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:21.030
are encoding new information."

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:22.170
End quote.

00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:24.990
Let me summarize this research.

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:28.070
Amateur chess players innovate.

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:32.300
Grandmasters remember.

00:24:32.300 --> 00:24:36.320
Outstanding achievement
involves knowing the past.

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:39.530
By immersing students in
dead languages and history,

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:45.740
the liberal arts aim at creating
grandmasters of citizenship.

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:47.300
Jefferson's view
of the importance

00:24:47.300 --> 00:24:50.810
of classical literature and
ancient history from the 1780s

00:24:50.810 --> 00:24:55.070
did not change after decades
spent as ambassador to France,

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:58.340
member of Washington's cabinet,
and two terms as president

00:24:58.340 --> 00:24:59.860
of the United States.

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:03.500
In Jack Rakove's words,
quote, "Managing a revolution

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:06.950
and conducting public affairs
at every level of government."

00:25:06.950 --> 00:25:07.970
End quote.

00:25:07.970 --> 00:25:10.820
Jefferson wrote to Nathaniel
Macon from Monticello

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:15.620
on January the 12, 1819, quote,
"I read no newspapers now

00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:18.990
but Ritchie's, and in that
chiefly the advertisements,

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:21.110
for they contain the
only truths to be

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:24.350
relied upon in a newspaper."

00:25:24.350 --> 00:25:27.710
I regret to say that Thomas
Jefferson sometimes criticized

00:25:27.710 --> 00:25:29.840
our great free press.

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:32.540
Jefferson-- we're all
shocked by that, so please--

00:25:32.540 --> 00:25:33.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:33.380 --> 00:25:35.150
--I understand.

00:25:35.150 --> 00:25:38.270
Jefferson went on to say, "I
feel a much greater interest

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:42.050
in knowing what has passed 2,000
or 3,000 years ago than what

00:25:42.050 --> 00:25:43.430
is now passing.

00:25:43.430 --> 00:25:45.320
I read nothing,
therefore, of the heroes

00:25:45.320 --> 00:25:48.890
of Troy, of the wars of
Lacedaemon and Athens,

00:25:48.890 --> 00:25:52.130
of Pompey and Caesar,
and of Augustus too."

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:53.780
End quote.

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:56.530
Clinton Rossiter in his
Seedtime of the Republic

00:25:56.530 --> 00:25:58.940
described the
founders' education.

00:25:58.940 --> 00:26:05.660
Quote, "It's easy to smile
at the dull, rigid, crabbed

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:09.020
methods that prevailed
in colonial colleges.

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:13.310
But if we judge the vineyards
by the fruit they brought forth,

00:26:13.310 --> 00:26:15.980
we must acknowledge
them of fertile ground,

00:26:15.980 --> 00:26:20.840
of learning, science,
reason, and liberty.

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:23.780
They may not have taught young
men enough useful knowledge,

00:26:23.780 --> 00:26:28.430
but they did teach them in
their own tradition-ridden way

00:26:28.430 --> 00:26:33.190
to think, communicate, and lead.

00:26:33.190 --> 00:26:36.970
To think, communicate,
and lead."

00:26:36.970 --> 00:26:40.730
And Rossiter goes on, "The roll
call of Harvard and William

00:26:40.730 --> 00:26:44.540
and Mary men in the revolution
should be evidence enough

00:26:44.540 --> 00:26:47.870
that Latin, logic, and
metaphysics, to which we

00:26:47.870 --> 00:26:52.120
may add ancient history,
were a rich fertilizer

00:26:52.120 --> 00:26:56.480
in the cultivation of
reason, virtue, honor,

00:26:56.480 --> 00:26:58.970
and love of liberty."

00:26:58.970 --> 00:27:02.090
Progressive educators focus
on liberating students

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:07.520
from the bondage of tradition
and the dead hand of the past.

00:27:07.520 --> 00:27:10.550
They reject studying the dead
languages that have given us

00:27:10.550 --> 00:27:15.890
the vocabulary for philosophy,
theology, medicine, science,

00:27:15.890 --> 00:27:18.530
law, and citizenship
and the history

00:27:18.530 --> 00:27:21.560
of the Athenian democracy,
the Roman Republic,

00:27:21.560 --> 00:27:23.600
and the American founding.

00:27:23.600 --> 00:27:27.170
George Orwell warned against
this attitude in his novel

00:27:27.170 --> 00:27:29.340
Nineteen Eighty-Four
when he described

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:33.020
the visit of Winston Smith
and his girlfriend Julia

00:27:33.020 --> 00:27:35.180
to the apartment of
the government agent

00:27:35.180 --> 00:27:37.910
O'Brien, who treats
them to good liquor

00:27:37.910 --> 00:27:40.940
and access to books
and conversation

00:27:40.940 --> 00:27:44.060
not available to most people.

00:27:44.060 --> 00:27:45.980
Sounds like an early
seminar of the Center

00:27:45.980 --> 00:27:49.800
for Constructive Alternatives.

00:27:49.800 --> 00:27:52.130
Eventually, there's
enough alcohol

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:53.450
left for one more toast.

00:27:53.450 --> 00:27:56.270
I hope we won't reach
that condition tonight.

00:27:56.270 --> 00:27:58.910
And O'Brien fills their glasses.

00:27:58.910 --> 00:28:00.380
"'What shall it be this time?'

00:28:00.380 --> 00:28:04.880
O'Brien said, still with
the same faint suggestion

00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:05.930
of irony."

00:28:05.930 --> 00:28:08.630
Seems to always have
this smirk on his face.

00:28:08.630 --> 00:28:11.000
"'To the confusion
of a thought police?

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:13.370
To the death of big brother?

00:28:13.370 --> 00:28:14.787
To humanity?

00:28:14.787 --> 00:28:15.980
Nah.

00:28:15.980 --> 00:28:22.620
To the future?'" "'To the
past,' said Winston Smith.

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:26.740
'Yes,' O'Brien agreed
gravely, no more smirking.

00:28:26.740 --> 00:28:29.880
'Yes,' O'Brien agreed gravely.

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:34.290
'The past is more important.'"

00:28:34.290 --> 00:28:38.610
George Orwell was a Democratic
socialist, not a conservative.

00:28:38.610 --> 00:28:40.650
But he was a
cultured man, and he

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:43.590
knew that the beauty
and freedom we enjoy

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:45.660
are legacies from the past.

00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:48.120
History and a liberal
arts education

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:51.970
teaches students to value
our traditions of language,

00:28:51.970 --> 00:28:54.210
history, and law,
and employ them

00:28:54.210 --> 00:28:57.960
to live as free citizens
of a nation founded

00:28:57.960 --> 00:29:01.290
with consensual institutions,
committed to debate

00:29:01.290 --> 00:29:04.680
and discussion, using
the lessons of history

00:29:04.680 --> 00:29:08.400
and the liberal arts of language
to persuade our fellow citizens

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:11.640
and devote ourselves
as our ancestors did

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:15.870
to the cultivation of
reason, virtue, honor,

00:29:15.870 --> 00:29:18.750
and love of liberty.

00:29:18.750 --> 00:29:21.720
Another reason to study history
in the liberal arts curriculum

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:25.410
is that these stories, whether
recounted orally or preserved

00:29:25.410 --> 00:29:28.500
in great works of literature,
touch our hearts in ways

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:31.020
that grammar and logic do not.

00:29:31.020 --> 00:29:34.500
The old Sunday school
hymn used to proclaim

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:39.090
"I love to tell this story
will be be my theme and glory

00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:44.130
to tell the old, old story
of Jesus and his love."

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:46.950
The hymn makes a point
that's valid for many stories

00:29:46.950 --> 00:29:49.890
in secular as well
as biblical history.

00:29:49.890 --> 00:29:54.420
"I love to tell the story
because I know it's true.

00:29:54.420 --> 00:30:01.280
It satisfies my longings
as nothing else can do."

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:03.500
The minister in Robert
Frost's "The Black Cottage"

00:30:03.500 --> 00:30:07.220
had an insight we need to hear.

00:30:07.220 --> 00:30:10.580
"Most of the change we
think we see in life

00:30:10.580 --> 00:30:14.180
is due to truths being
in and out of favor.

00:30:14.180 --> 00:30:17.600
As I said here and
oftentimes, I wish

00:30:17.600 --> 00:30:20.510
I could be monarch
of a desert land.

00:30:20.510 --> 00:30:23.570
I would devote and
dedicate forever

00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:29.880
to the truths we keep
coming back and back to."

00:30:29.880 --> 00:30:33.960
I like that poem, but I
do have a quibble with it.

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:38.820
Lands that are dedicated to
the truths and stories "we

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:43.870
keep coming back and
back to" are not deserts.

00:30:43.870 --> 00:30:48.180
They're full of family farms,
local factories, and churches.

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:50.310
Their families have
a lot of children.

00:30:50.310 --> 00:30:52.860
Their citizens are soldiers.

00:30:52.860 --> 00:30:55.890
Their story is our story.

00:30:55.890 --> 00:30:58.110
Today we like to
proclaim, nothing's

00:30:58.110 --> 00:31:01.080
the same after some
important event.

00:31:01.080 --> 00:31:02.730
If we have to hear
about the past,

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:05.340
we want it laid out
for us in PowerPoint

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:11.040
or downloaded onto our Kindle so
we can skim it on the airplane.

00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:14.400
Give us the gist, and
if you don't mind,

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:17.660
spare us the history lesson.

00:31:17.660 --> 00:31:20.270
Traditional liberal
arts education, however,

00:31:20.270 --> 00:31:22.850
insisted on the history
lesson, beginning

00:31:22.850 --> 00:31:25.220
with the ancient
Greeks and Romans.

00:31:25.220 --> 00:31:28.190
What most Americans
value about America--

00:31:28.190 --> 00:31:32.510
consensual institutions, the
rule of law, private property,

00:31:32.510 --> 00:31:34.340
science, faith--

00:31:34.340 --> 00:31:37.550
cannot survive without the
ancient traditions that

00:31:37.550 --> 00:31:40.940
inspired and maintained them.

00:31:40.940 --> 00:31:43.760
We are free and
creative not because we

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:48.770
inhabit a new nation
dedicated to a proposition,

00:31:48.770 --> 00:31:52.640
as somebody once said, but
because we are citizens

00:31:52.640 --> 00:31:54.680
of what General MacArthur
called the United

00:31:54.680 --> 00:31:58.340
States in his last great
speech at West Point.

00:31:58.340 --> 00:32:05.930
Quote, "This beloved land of
culture and ancient descent."

00:32:05.930 --> 00:32:08.840
"Culture and ancient descent."

00:32:08.840 --> 00:32:10.190
End quote.

00:32:10.190 --> 00:32:12.950
John Adams wrote his
friend, Dr. Benjamin Rush,

00:32:12.950 --> 00:32:15.740
"I should as soon think
of closing all my window

00:32:15.740 --> 00:32:20.240
shutters to enable me to see
as of banishing the classics

00:32:20.240 --> 00:32:23.350
to improve Republican ideas."

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:26.560
The liberally educated value
of the past and the great

00:32:26.560 --> 00:32:29.770
works that preserve that past.

00:32:29.770 --> 00:32:33.790
"We all want progress," CS
Lewis told his radio audience

00:32:33.790 --> 00:32:35.470
in 1942.

00:32:35.470 --> 00:32:37.870
"But progress means
getting nearer

00:32:37.870 --> 00:32:40.630
to the place you want to be.

00:32:40.630 --> 00:32:43.150
If you're on the
wrong road, progress

00:32:43.150 --> 00:32:47.660
means doing an about turn and
walking back to the right road.

00:32:47.660 --> 00:32:51.100
And in that case, the man
who turns back soonest

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:53.110
is the most progressive."

00:32:53.110 --> 00:32:54.950
End quote.

00:32:54.950 --> 00:32:58.070
It's not a question of
choosing the right major.

00:32:58.070 --> 00:33:00.200
We're on the wrong road.

00:33:00.200 --> 00:33:03.200
We've privileged job training
over the liberal arts,

00:33:03.200 --> 00:33:07.400
global free trade over family
farms and local businesses,

00:33:07.400 --> 00:33:10.730
technology over great
books, the consensus

00:33:10.730 --> 00:33:15.350
of self-proclaimed experts over
the wisdom of our ancestors.

00:33:15.350 --> 00:33:19.820
It's time to turn around and
go back to the right road.

00:33:19.820 --> 00:33:25.530
We shall recognize it when we
get there, because, you see,

00:33:25.530 --> 00:33:28.223
it's the road home.

00:33:28.223 --> 00:33:29.640
Thank you for your
kind attention.

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:33.875
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:40.847 --> 00:33:42.180
SPEAKER 2: Thank you, Dr. Kopff.

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:45.590
We now have time for Q&amp;A,
so if you have a question,

00:33:45.590 --> 00:33:47.620
please make your way to
one of the microphones.

00:34:00.400 --> 00:34:02.800
I have to remind students
that good questions will

00:34:02.800 --> 00:34:04.600
be considered for extra credit.

00:34:04.600 --> 00:34:06.772
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:06.772 --> 00:34:08.230
E. CHRISTIAN KOPFF:
That's bribery.

00:34:08.230 --> 00:34:10.732
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:10.732 --> 00:34:12.690
AUDIENCE: Thank you very
much for [INAUDIBLE]..

00:34:12.690 --> 00:34:17.909
How do you suggest the
[INAUDIBLE] understanding

00:34:17.909 --> 00:34:20.300
how it applies to
us, how we use it?

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:22.739
How do we incorporate
that in today?

00:34:22.739 --> 00:34:26.320
How do we inspire those who
are not inspired [INAUDIBLE] we

00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:28.550
understood [INAUDIBLE]?

00:34:28.550 --> 00:34:30.550
E. CHRISTIAN KOPFF: Yes,
that's a good question,

00:34:30.550 --> 00:34:32.320
and I don't have a
good answer for it.

00:34:32.320 --> 00:34:36.120
One way you-- and my impression
of it, inspiring students--

00:34:36.120 --> 00:34:37.989
and there actually is
research supporting

00:34:37.989 --> 00:34:41.120
it is that love what you teach.

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:45.440
Be enthusiastic about
your country and its story

00:34:45.440 --> 00:34:48.030
and the stories
that lie behind it.

00:34:48.030 --> 00:34:49.909
I had a friend in
our education school

00:34:49.909 --> 00:34:53.719
whose research showed that the
most effective part of teaching

00:34:53.719 --> 00:34:57.320
more than knowledge
and more than skill

00:34:57.320 --> 00:35:00.440
was thinking your
subject was important.

00:35:00.440 --> 00:35:02.360
And he said, this is bizarre.

00:35:02.360 --> 00:35:06.560
So the one thing we can't teach
is the most important thing,

00:35:06.560 --> 00:35:08.030
is thinking it matters.

00:35:08.030 --> 00:35:12.170
Once that happens, especially
when you rely on the fact

00:35:12.170 --> 00:35:16.910
that these people, the people
that our founders found

00:35:16.910 --> 00:35:19.930
important were the people that
the people the Reformation era

00:35:19.930 --> 00:35:22.550
found important, the people
that Dante and Petrarch

00:35:22.550 --> 00:35:24.290
found important.

00:35:24.290 --> 00:35:28.260
Ezra Pound says somewhere,
writings and people

00:35:28.260 --> 00:35:30.780
that have been found to be
interesting and important

00:35:30.780 --> 00:35:35.130
for thousands of years are
probably not very boring.

00:35:35.130 --> 00:35:39.440
And we sort of need to have
confidence that this is true.

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:40.812
There's a sense that--

00:35:40.812 --> 00:35:42.270
especially when
you teach classics,

00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:43.232
you really notice this.

00:35:43.232 --> 00:35:45.690
When is the day going to come
when people are going to say,

00:35:45.690 --> 00:35:46.890
this is ridiculous?

00:35:46.890 --> 00:35:49.280
This was thousands of years ago.

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:53.270
How can this possibly
matter to us now?

00:35:53.270 --> 00:35:56.630
And the answer is you sort of
need to believe that it does.

00:35:56.630 --> 00:35:58.970
And John Dickinson's
point is the one

00:35:58.970 --> 00:36:00.590
that really is relevant here.

00:36:00.590 --> 00:36:05.690
This is what has made sense for
people for a long, long time.

00:36:05.690 --> 00:36:08.180
And it's not just John
Dickinson in the 18th century.

00:36:08.180 --> 00:36:11.030
Frankly, the argument
that Dickinson uses--

00:36:11.030 --> 00:36:12.620
the argument about
accidents probably

00:36:12.620 --> 00:36:15.590
produced this and
experience showed,

00:36:15.590 --> 00:36:17.180
history showed that it worked--

00:36:17.180 --> 00:36:20.870
is the same argument used by the
great biologist Konrad Lorenz

00:36:20.870 --> 00:36:27.950
in his book on mankind's mortal
sins where he as a biologist

00:36:27.950 --> 00:36:33.620
compares the traditions, what
one of mankind's mortal sins is

00:36:33.620 --> 00:36:36.870
[NON-ENGLISH],,
destroying tradition.

00:36:36.870 --> 00:36:39.065
And he says, tradition
of our ethical

00:36:39.065 --> 00:36:42.050
and our religious traditions
and our political traditions

00:36:42.050 --> 00:36:45.230
too, I would add, he views
like the human genome, which

00:36:45.230 --> 00:36:47.270
he didn't think was
created or designed.

00:36:47.270 --> 00:36:50.690
He thought it happened because
accidents caused mutations

00:36:50.690 --> 00:36:52.550
and good mutations survived.

00:36:52.550 --> 00:36:55.500
But it comes to the same thing.

00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:57.120
And he says the
great traditions,

00:36:57.120 --> 00:36:59.610
the ethical and religious
traditions of mankind

00:36:59.610 --> 00:37:02.730
have the same validity
that our genome does,

00:37:02.730 --> 00:37:05.640
and nobody could invent
it, either one group

00:37:05.640 --> 00:37:06.880
or the other group.

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:09.300
What you need to
do is be confident

00:37:09.300 --> 00:37:11.730
that that's true when
you're looking at it.

00:37:11.730 --> 00:37:13.380
Don't look at it
as something that--

00:37:13.380 --> 00:37:15.360
and this is the problem
I have with Martha

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:16.320
Nussbaum, of course.

00:37:16.320 --> 00:37:20.140
She assumes that
the past is wrong

00:37:20.140 --> 00:37:22.330
and that tradition
is irrational instead

00:37:22.330 --> 00:37:25.475
of assuming that something
that's made sense for so long

00:37:25.475 --> 00:37:27.850
is going to continue to make
sense if we just give people

00:37:27.850 --> 00:37:29.312
a chance to hear it.

00:37:29.312 --> 00:37:30.490
Does that respond to it?

00:37:30.490 --> 00:37:31.220
Yes?

00:37:31.220 --> 00:37:31.720
Thank you.

00:37:37.850 --> 00:37:40.490
AUDIENCE: Thank you so much for
your wisdom and exhortation.

00:37:40.490 --> 00:37:42.140
I'm wondering, for
those of us who

00:37:42.140 --> 00:37:44.660
strive and desire to
be scholars of history,

00:37:44.660 --> 00:37:47.970
when we see history
beginning to repeat itself

00:37:47.970 --> 00:37:51.560
and when history itself is the
best testimonial to those who

00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:55.550
are deaf to its very
prophecy, is there any room,

00:37:55.550 --> 00:37:58.520
or how much room is
there for compromise when

00:37:58.520 --> 00:38:05.420
it comes to our culture's desire
for STEM answers, I suppose?

00:38:05.420 --> 00:38:07.370
When people don't want
to listen to history,

00:38:07.370 --> 00:38:10.460
but history holds the answers,
how much room is there

00:38:10.460 --> 00:38:12.320
for us to compromise
when it comes

00:38:12.320 --> 00:38:14.570
to warning them of
the very thing that

00:38:14.570 --> 00:38:15.795
is likely to repeat itself?

00:38:15.795 --> 00:38:17.420
E. CHRISTIAN KOPFF:
Well, here, I mean,

00:38:17.420 --> 00:38:19.460
you bring up a
really good point.

00:38:19.460 --> 00:38:25.700
And the fact is we have an
unfair advantage, because they

00:38:25.700 --> 00:38:26.640
have to write the--

00:38:26.640 --> 00:38:29.060
you've all seen Karl
Rove writing these things

00:38:29.060 --> 00:38:31.760
on the whiteboard in
numbers and on and on.

00:38:31.760 --> 00:38:36.120
But the fact is people
respond to stories

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:41.390
and heroes and villains much
more than they do to numbers.

00:38:41.390 --> 00:38:44.720
Now sometimes that's wrong,
and sometimes the numbers

00:38:44.720 --> 00:38:46.580
tell us things we
need to listen to.

00:38:46.580 --> 00:38:49.160
But for the most
part, we get to bring

00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:51.680
in the heroes and the
villains and the stories that

00:38:51.680 --> 00:38:57.590
really make sense to more
people and to ourselves also.

00:38:57.590 --> 00:39:00.680
The really wonderful book on
the founders and the classics

00:39:00.680 --> 00:39:04.660
has a wonderful title, which is
The Founders and the Classics.

00:39:04.660 --> 00:39:08.420
And it's by a great-- wonderful
scholar named Carl Richard.

00:39:08.420 --> 00:39:10.550
And it's spelled Richard,
but he's a Cajun,

00:39:10.550 --> 00:39:14.270
and he, like the French,
leave off the last letter.

00:39:14.270 --> 00:39:19.040
And his book includes a chapter
on the heroes and the villains.

00:39:19.040 --> 00:39:20.540
Who are the heroes
and the villains?

00:39:20.540 --> 00:39:23.280
And some of these, of
course, are people that

00:39:23.280 --> 00:39:25.610
change places at other times.

00:39:25.610 --> 00:39:29.510
Julius Caesar was a villain
back in the 18th century.

00:39:29.510 --> 00:39:33.395
100 years later, the great
liberal classicist Theodor

00:39:33.395 --> 00:39:36.290
Mommsen, Caesar was a hero.

00:39:36.290 --> 00:39:39.230
And for Dante,
Brutus and Cassius

00:39:39.230 --> 00:39:40.590
are in the lowest part of Hell.

00:39:40.590 --> 00:39:43.490
They're right next
to Judas because they

00:39:43.490 --> 00:39:46.580
were disloyal to their emperor.

00:39:46.580 --> 00:39:48.933
So the heroes and villains
may change sometimes

00:39:48.933 --> 00:39:49.850
for different reasons.

00:39:49.850 --> 00:39:53.660
But for the most
part, these are things

00:39:53.660 --> 00:39:57.430
we can make to come alive for
people because they were alive.

00:39:57.430 --> 00:39:59.350
So I think we have
the unfair advantage,

00:39:59.350 --> 00:40:02.470
if we'll take advantage
of it and we'll insist,

00:40:02.470 --> 00:40:04.730
wait a second.

00:40:04.730 --> 00:40:06.560
I'm going to
believe those models

00:40:06.560 --> 00:40:09.020
when it comes out that way.

00:40:09.020 --> 00:40:11.906
But they don't all
come out that way.

00:40:11.906 --> 00:40:13.860
Does that make sense?

00:40:13.860 --> 00:40:14.360
Thank you.

00:40:18.330 --> 00:40:20.350
AUDIENCE: Thank
you for your time.

00:40:20.350 --> 00:40:24.320
I was curious about
tradition, because it

00:40:24.320 --> 00:40:30.450
seems like when we refer
to tradition, [INAUDIBLE]

00:40:30.450 --> 00:40:34.060
there's a golden age that
we sort of look back to.

00:40:34.060 --> 00:40:35.360
Is that fair to say?

00:40:35.360 --> 00:40:38.230
Or is there a sort of
standard that we look back to?

00:40:38.230 --> 00:40:42.020
Because it seems as if almost
every generation [INAUDIBLE]

00:40:42.020 --> 00:40:43.680
tradition.

00:40:43.680 --> 00:40:47.610
I guess, where are we
supposed to look back

00:40:47.610 --> 00:40:49.820
to as sort of the standard?

00:40:52.570 --> 00:40:57.030
Like, will we look back to just
as far as the American founding

00:40:57.030 --> 00:41:01.770
or back to the middle
ages or farther?

00:41:01.770 --> 00:41:03.390
E. CHRISTIAN KOPFF: Right.

00:41:03.390 --> 00:41:04.230
Great question.

00:41:04.230 --> 00:41:13.650
And the success or the argument
for the great traditions

00:41:13.650 --> 00:41:18.300
is precisely that they've
been through so many ages.

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:21.180
And one of the points that
Konrad Lorenz makes even more

00:41:21.180 --> 00:41:26.880
clearly than John Dickinson
is that no one person or study

00:41:26.880 --> 00:41:30.450
group or generation
could come up

00:41:30.450 --> 00:41:35.460
with the human genome or
the great moral traditions.

00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:40.980
Anyone who's read CS Lewis's
book we've got quoted here,

00:41:40.980 --> 00:41:46.620
he lists all the commonalities
of ethical tradition

00:41:46.620 --> 00:41:49.710
all over the world in
The Abolition of Man.

00:41:49.710 --> 00:41:55.230
And the argument is precisely
that no one generation

00:41:55.230 --> 00:41:56.760
has it all.

00:41:56.760 --> 00:41:59.280
Tennyson says in his
Arthurian poem somewhere,

00:41:59.280 --> 00:42:01.970
"The old order change is
making way for the new,

00:42:01.970 --> 00:42:04.710
and God fulfills
himself in many ways,

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:08.760
lest one good custom
should destroy the world."

00:42:08.760 --> 00:42:12.900
There not only is not a pure
golden age, but they're--

00:42:12.900 --> 00:42:15.780
the whole point of these
traditions-- ethical,

00:42:15.780 --> 00:42:19.730
political, et cetera,
or religious--

00:42:19.730 --> 00:42:23.780
is precisely they've been
through so many different ages.

00:42:23.780 --> 00:42:27.830
Now I mean, I don't want
to present an argument

00:42:27.830 --> 00:42:30.770
from Christianity that doesn't
involve the Holy Ghost,

00:42:30.770 --> 00:42:33.230
obviously, but here's
something that's

00:42:33.230 --> 00:42:35.270
been through
antiquity, the Middle

00:42:35.270 --> 00:42:37.710
Ages, and the modern world.

00:42:37.710 --> 00:42:44.360
It's known a lot more than
anybody now could know.

00:42:44.360 --> 00:42:47.110
Now, let me just
modify what I just

00:42:47.110 --> 00:42:52.450
said by bringing up the point we
had about heroes and villains.

00:42:52.450 --> 00:42:55.200
Every healthy society,
every healthy person

00:42:55.200 --> 00:42:57.340
has somebody who views
as a kind of hero,

00:42:57.340 --> 00:43:00.340
and every healthy society
has some age that they

00:43:00.340 --> 00:43:02.680
view is as a wonderful age.

00:43:02.680 --> 00:43:06.280
But when you honor it
and you honor those,

00:43:06.280 --> 00:43:07.870
you honor them for
the good things

00:43:07.870 --> 00:43:09.885
and you know there
are bad things too.

00:43:09.885 --> 00:43:11.260
Whereas the great
traditions that

00:43:11.260 --> 00:43:16.430
have made their way through
so many ages, so many peoples,

00:43:16.430 --> 00:43:19.090
and so many times,
that's the thing

00:43:19.090 --> 00:43:22.030
that I would argue for
in favor of tradition.

00:43:22.030 --> 00:43:24.130
And why I would argue
again for history,

00:43:24.130 --> 00:43:27.490
because history allows us to
look when these things worked.

00:43:27.490 --> 00:43:30.920
And are there times
when they didn't work?

00:43:30.920 --> 00:43:32.580
Does that make sense?

00:43:32.580 --> 00:43:34.587
Thank you.

00:43:34.587 --> 00:43:36.583
AUDIENCE: Thank you for
speaking to us today.

00:43:36.583 --> 00:43:38.080
So I had a question.

00:43:38.080 --> 00:43:44.325
You were referring a lot to how
the founders took into context

00:43:44.325 --> 00:43:46.500
these greats from the past.

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:49.350
And all of them paid attention
to many of the same people.

00:43:49.350 --> 00:43:51.600
But even among the
founders, these people who

00:43:51.600 --> 00:43:54.270
are reading the same works
from the same people,

00:43:54.270 --> 00:43:56.100
they had different--

00:43:56.100 --> 00:43:57.780
not even
interpretations, but you

00:43:57.780 --> 00:44:00.480
could say different applications
of the same lessons.

00:44:00.480 --> 00:44:05.630
Can you speak to how, even
though lessons from history

00:44:05.630 --> 00:44:09.800
are unchanging, our
interpretations of them

00:44:09.800 --> 00:44:13.640
are changing, how do we know
how to apply history correctly

00:44:13.640 --> 00:44:16.370
and not in a twisted
manner, maybe twisted

00:44:16.370 --> 00:44:19.945
by our own personal experiences
or the age in which we live?

00:44:19.945 --> 00:44:21.570
E. CHRISTIAN KOPFF:
So I'm going to let

00:44:21.570 --> 00:44:24.060
Aristotle answer this question.

00:44:24.060 --> 00:44:27.720
And one of his points-- and
he makes this in the ethics

00:44:27.720 --> 00:44:31.530
and he makes it On The Soul--
is that there are people that

00:44:31.530 --> 00:44:35.160
are experts in a little
area, and they're

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:36.600
really good in that area.

00:44:36.600 --> 00:44:40.890
But they can't connect it to
the wider society they live in.

00:44:40.890 --> 00:44:43.170
And for Aristotle
and for Plato, there

00:44:43.170 --> 00:44:47.230
are these architectonic visions.

00:44:47.230 --> 00:44:50.040
And for example, for
Aristotle, economics

00:44:50.040 --> 00:44:52.490
is subordinate to politics.

00:44:52.490 --> 00:44:56.440
I know many people who think
it shouldn't be that way.

00:44:56.440 --> 00:45:00.300
Economists should be telling
the politicians what to do.

00:45:00.300 --> 00:45:03.270
But the idea that there's a--

00:45:03.270 --> 00:45:07.390
the point is that a
liberally educated person,

00:45:07.390 --> 00:45:09.700
a person educated
to be a citizen

00:45:09.700 --> 00:45:13.000
has that kind of
education, how to use

00:45:13.000 --> 00:45:16.570
the opinions of smart people.

00:45:16.570 --> 00:45:21.100
So I will say a couple of
things that I said today

00:45:21.100 --> 00:45:24.870
the leading scholars in
the field don't agree with.

00:45:24.870 --> 00:45:25.660
OK?

00:45:25.660 --> 00:45:27.760
In fact, there's a
genuine consensus

00:45:27.760 --> 00:45:30.240
that the founders are--

00:45:30.240 --> 00:45:32.940
the founding was an
Enlightenment project,

00:45:32.940 --> 00:45:35.400
and that it was a
distinctly modern project.

00:45:35.400 --> 00:45:37.740
And I just don't
mean the other side.

00:45:37.740 --> 00:45:41.850
I mean people on our side,
really good people on our side.

00:45:41.850 --> 00:45:43.940
So I mean, it's legitimate.

00:45:43.940 --> 00:45:46.710
I happen to think
the evidence when

00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:49.890
evaluated carefully
and critically

00:45:49.890 --> 00:45:53.040
would show that somebody's
right and somebody isn't right.

00:45:53.040 --> 00:45:55.410
But still, there's legitimate
points there to be made.

00:45:55.410 --> 00:45:58.230
And by the way, people
who thought that Americans

00:45:58.230 --> 00:46:05.610
threw away their old prejudices
and created a new society,

00:46:05.610 --> 00:46:09.540
there's de Crevecoeur's
1782 work Letters

00:46:09.540 --> 00:46:12.555
From an American Farmer
where he says exactly that.

00:46:12.555 --> 00:46:14.640
He sounds exactly
like Bernard Bailyn

00:46:14.640 --> 00:46:18.510
and Jack Rakove and Martha
Nussbaum back in 1782.

00:46:18.510 --> 00:46:21.210
Now, I think there were a lot
more people like John Dickinson

00:46:21.210 --> 00:46:22.140
and Thomas Jefferson.

00:46:22.140 --> 00:46:26.210
But still, part of a
liberal arts education

00:46:26.210 --> 00:46:29.450
is that you with a good
liberal arts education

00:46:29.450 --> 00:46:32.810
can decide what
the experts who are

00:46:32.810 --> 00:46:35.210
number one in their
teeny area but can't

00:46:35.210 --> 00:46:40.200
see the whole picture,
how this fits into things.

00:46:40.200 --> 00:46:42.168
So I mean, if I'm right--

00:46:42.168 --> 00:46:44.210
if I'm wrong, of course,
then we should hand over

00:46:44.210 --> 00:46:49.910
our lives to Dr. Fauci or
whoever the expert is, right?

00:46:49.910 --> 00:46:50.540
You laugh.

00:46:50.540 --> 00:46:52.207
There are parts of
Boulder that wouldn't

00:46:52.207 --> 00:46:55.850
be laughing at that comment.

00:46:55.850 --> 00:46:58.520
But the fact-- it's the job
of the liberally educated,

00:46:58.520 --> 00:47:01.070
the people who have
a vision of history,

00:47:01.070 --> 00:47:05.300
of language, of philosophy,
of literature too

00:47:05.300 --> 00:47:08.900
who should be judging what
the experts in little areas

00:47:08.900 --> 00:47:10.795
are telling us.

00:47:10.795 --> 00:47:13.170
So that ultimately, there is
a way to get a right answer,

00:47:13.170 --> 00:47:18.300
but the openness to
debate is not a bad thing.

00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:20.720
That's one of the things
we should be learning

00:47:20.720 --> 00:47:23.330
from the study of the past.

00:47:23.330 --> 00:47:24.543
Does that make sense?

00:47:24.543 --> 00:47:25.036
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:25.036 --> 00:47:25.536
Thank you.

00:47:25.536 --> 00:47:27.990
E. CHRISTIAN KOPFF: Thank you.

00:47:27.990 --> 00:47:29.740
AUDIENCE: Thank you
so much for your talk.

00:47:29.740 --> 00:47:32.100
This is [INAUDIBLE] what
you were just talking about,

00:47:32.100 --> 00:47:34.720
but what are your thoughts
on the idea of allowing

00:47:34.720 --> 00:47:38.610
or requiring students to
major in a certain subject

00:47:38.610 --> 00:47:40.900
[INAUDIBLE] college.

00:47:40.900 --> 00:47:44.350
[INAUDIBLE] say that
that's not [INAUDIBLE]..

00:47:44.350 --> 00:47:46.680
And then also, are
there certain majors

00:47:46.680 --> 00:47:50.590
that don't really fit with
a liberal arts education?

00:47:50.590 --> 00:47:52.940
E. CHRISTIAN KOPFF: So it's
a really good question.

00:47:52.940 --> 00:47:55.270
I don't have a really
good answer for it.

00:47:55.270 --> 00:48:00.200
The idea of majors is something
of a concession to a developing

00:48:00.200 --> 00:48:02.120
situation.

00:48:02.120 --> 00:48:03.950
The advantage of
departments is they

00:48:03.950 --> 00:48:07.430
allow a certain kind of academic
standard to be maintained,

00:48:07.430 --> 00:48:10.040
and the disadvantage
is they tend

00:48:10.040 --> 00:48:11.810
to leave out things
that don't fit

00:48:11.810 --> 00:48:15.600
into their narrow
academic specialty.

00:48:15.600 --> 00:48:17.300
Are there things
that shouldn't be

00:48:17.300 --> 00:48:21.800
part of an undergraduate
liberal arts education?

00:48:21.800 --> 00:48:23.870
Well, all we have to do
is go to the same issue

00:48:23.870 --> 00:48:27.290
of the American History
Association's Perspectives

00:48:27.290 --> 00:48:31.350
on History and look at the ones
that are doing well since 2008.

00:48:31.350 --> 00:48:34.400
They're the ones that
shouldn't be there.

00:48:34.400 --> 00:48:38.020
Or go on to Forbes, and Forbes
will say, the majors that

00:48:38.020 --> 00:48:40.050
give you the best first job.

00:48:40.050 --> 00:48:40.550
Right?

00:48:40.550 --> 00:48:41.530
And list these things.

00:48:41.530 --> 00:48:43.970
And it's computer science
and it's all kinds

00:48:43.970 --> 00:48:45.620
of engineering things.

00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:48.680
And I'm saying that a little
bit carefully, because actually,

00:48:48.680 --> 00:48:51.530
classics is in the
top 20 in the list.

00:48:51.530 --> 00:48:54.590
With a classics major,
you actually, as a rule--

00:48:54.590 --> 00:48:56.708
according to Forbes, I
don't know this myself--

00:48:56.708 --> 00:48:57.750
but according to Forbes--

00:48:57.750 --> 00:48:58.990
who would question Forbes?

00:48:58.990 --> 00:49:01.970
Not I, certainly.

00:49:01.970 --> 00:49:04.030
Think that classic majors--
now, they're top 20.

00:49:04.030 --> 00:49:04.822
They're not top 10.

00:49:04.822 --> 00:49:07.880
The top 10 are all
computer science stuff.

00:49:07.880 --> 00:49:10.490
And I also get this
because I'm always having

00:49:10.490 --> 00:49:12.080
trouble with my computer.

00:49:12.080 --> 00:49:14.390
And I go into the
library where they are,

00:49:14.390 --> 00:49:16.430
and there's the kids fixing it.

00:49:16.430 --> 00:49:18.260
They're all undergraduates.

00:49:18.260 --> 00:49:20.570
And I remember one telling me.

00:49:20.570 --> 00:49:22.120
After he told me
this, I asked others

00:49:22.120 --> 00:49:23.340
and it turned out to be true.

00:49:23.340 --> 00:49:26.270
When I came here, I was
going to be a bio major.

00:49:26.270 --> 00:49:30.210
Then I found out only about 20%
of them even get to med school.

00:49:30.210 --> 00:49:33.470
So I had, by the way, provoked
the statement that I just

00:49:33.470 --> 00:49:36.740
mentioned from the
colleague, confirming

00:49:36.740 --> 00:49:38.030
what the student had heard.

00:49:38.030 --> 00:49:41.570
So I switched to
computer science,

00:49:41.570 --> 00:49:44.660
because that way I'm guaranteed
to get a good first job.

00:49:44.660 --> 00:49:45.423
And he's right.

00:49:45.423 --> 00:49:46.840
Guaranteed is too
much, of course.

00:49:46.840 --> 00:49:51.172
But it is-- it's better
than one in five.

00:49:51.172 --> 00:49:53.030
It's better tan eeny,
meeny, miny, moe,

00:49:53.030 --> 00:49:55.610
which is sort of what
you get the other way.

00:49:55.610 --> 00:49:57.170
So I mean, those
are the subjects

00:49:57.170 --> 00:49:59.330
we shouldn't be teaching.

00:49:59.330 --> 00:50:01.250
My school-- I went to
a small liberal arts

00:50:01.250 --> 00:50:02.660
school, Haverford College.

00:50:02.660 --> 00:50:04.130
500 men.

00:50:04.130 --> 00:50:05.870
Smaller than you guys.

00:50:05.870 --> 00:50:07.370
By the way, it's
bloated since then.

00:50:07.370 --> 00:50:08.690
They let in women.

00:50:08.690 --> 00:50:11.660
It's now 1,100, so it's bloated.

00:50:11.660 --> 00:50:15.310
But they just sent us propaganda
to make us give more money.

00:50:15.310 --> 00:50:18.170
And one of the arguments
was 10 years ago,

00:50:18.170 --> 00:50:22.670
there was one computer science
major, and now there are 40.

00:50:22.670 --> 00:50:24.688
Isn't this good?

00:50:24.688 --> 00:50:26.480
And I thought, well,
you know, it certainly

00:50:26.480 --> 00:50:28.010
makes it easier on
my checkbook when

00:50:28.010 --> 00:50:30.650
you're telling me things
which I'm completely

00:50:30.650 --> 00:50:32.180
opposed to on principle.

00:50:32.180 --> 00:50:33.637
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:33.637 --> 00:50:35.720
These people, they should
be studying mathematics.

00:50:35.720 --> 00:50:37.130
They should be studying science.

00:50:37.130 --> 00:50:39.620
They shouldn't be studying
at a liberal arts school.

00:50:39.620 --> 00:50:42.710
They should be learning
to be the Carly

00:50:42.710 --> 00:50:46.550
Fiorinas of these
businesses in 10 and 15

00:50:46.550 --> 00:50:52.060
years, which is what you guys
are going to be, by the way.

00:50:52.060 --> 00:50:54.540
You can tell your parents
that anyhow if you want to.

00:50:54.540 --> 00:50:57.720
I once was teaching
a course for one

00:50:57.720 --> 00:50:59.200
of our courses done in a dorm.

00:50:59.200 --> 00:51:00.190
We were trying to--

00:51:00.190 --> 00:51:03.173
thought it was a great idea to
teach people in their dorms.

00:51:03.173 --> 00:51:04.590
And it was a Greek
history course,

00:51:04.590 --> 00:51:07.510
and two kids came up to me
after about a month and said,

00:51:07.510 --> 00:51:09.660
ancient history is so great.

00:51:09.660 --> 00:51:12.270
I was going to be
a physics major,

00:51:12.270 --> 00:51:14.880
but I'm thinking of
becoming a history major.

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:20.670
And I said, one, you should
follow what you find important.

00:51:20.670 --> 00:51:23.460
Two, your parents are going
to hire a hitman from Miami

00:51:23.460 --> 00:51:24.000
to kill me.

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:25.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:25.860 --> 00:51:28.650
Because they're going to
think you've committed career

00:51:28.650 --> 00:51:31.890
suicide by leaving a science--

00:51:31.890 --> 00:51:34.820
a STEM subject and
going into this.

00:51:34.820 --> 00:51:37.560
So please warn me if you
see large sums of money

00:51:37.560 --> 00:51:42.420
on your parent's credit cards
going to flights from Miami

00:51:42.420 --> 00:51:46.215
to Denver, because I'll know
to start protecting myself.

00:51:49.080 --> 00:51:51.210
You know, basically,
the more you

00:51:51.210 --> 00:51:53.790
go back to the older
liberal arts education,

00:51:53.790 --> 00:51:55.620
this is the education
that gave us

00:51:55.620 --> 00:51:59.430
Dante, that gave us Luther and
Calvin, that gave us Aquinas

00:51:59.430 --> 00:52:01.110
and William of Ockham, frankly.

00:52:01.110 --> 00:52:03.300
They gave us the founders.

00:52:03.300 --> 00:52:08.870
And this is the education
that prepares you to think,

00:52:08.870 --> 00:52:11.150
communicate, and lead.

00:52:11.150 --> 00:52:13.700
And that's where things
like computer science

00:52:13.700 --> 00:52:17.020
are distracting.

00:52:17.020 --> 00:52:17.520
Thank you.

00:52:17.520 --> 00:52:18.540
I hope that answers.

00:52:18.540 --> 00:52:22.110
SPEAKER 2: We have time
for one last question.

00:52:22.110 --> 00:52:24.730
AUDIENCE: Thank you for
speaking to us today.

00:52:24.730 --> 00:52:28.080
My question is, have
you seen a movement back

00:52:28.080 --> 00:52:32.490
towards the liberal arts
or to history in general

00:52:32.490 --> 00:52:35.730
in any of your colleagues
or in academia?

00:52:35.730 --> 00:52:39.090
Or do you feel like
you're sort of alone,

00:52:39.090 --> 00:52:40.585
standing in this
fight for history?

00:52:40.585 --> 00:52:42.210
E. CHRISTIAN KOPFF:
Well, fortunately--

00:52:42.210 --> 00:52:45.470
I mean, if you do
your job properly--

00:52:45.470 --> 00:52:49.170
and understand that teaching
is part of your job--

00:52:49.170 --> 00:52:51.440
and you're excited
about your subject,

00:52:51.440 --> 00:52:53.700
there are going to be majors.

00:52:53.700 --> 00:52:57.480
And the languages, all
of them, the living ones

00:52:57.480 --> 00:52:59.910
except for Spanish as
well as the dead ones

00:52:59.910 --> 00:53:03.870
have had their problems.

00:53:03.870 --> 00:53:07.260
Shortly after that report
from the Census Bureau

00:53:07.260 --> 00:53:12.360
came out talking about 74%
of STEM undergraduate degrees

00:53:12.360 --> 00:53:18.090
didn't end up doing STEM,
one of the leftist journals

00:53:18.090 --> 00:53:19.450
went and investigated it.

00:53:19.450 --> 00:53:21.500
And now I can't remember
which one it was.

00:53:21.500 --> 00:53:24.150
And what they discovered
was that at Yale,

00:53:24.150 --> 00:53:25.890
history was very popular.

00:53:25.890 --> 00:53:28.330
And I think it was the
third most popular major.

00:53:28.330 --> 00:53:30.750
And at Princeton, they were
having so many students

00:53:30.750 --> 00:53:32.550
they were hiring.

00:53:32.550 --> 00:53:34.620
That's what we
really care about.

00:53:34.620 --> 00:53:36.740
Who's getting the
faculty positions, right?

00:53:36.740 --> 00:53:37.650
On and on.

00:53:37.650 --> 00:53:42.120
And their theory was, you see
who goes to Yale and Princeton

00:53:42.120 --> 00:53:43.680
but the rich people
who are going

00:53:43.680 --> 00:53:45.750
to oppress the rest of us.

00:53:45.750 --> 00:53:48.870
And they were cleverly
learning about the past

00:53:48.870 --> 00:53:50.730
and learning about
these traditions

00:53:50.730 --> 00:53:54.030
and having the poor proles
study these subjects that

00:53:54.030 --> 00:53:58.050
led directly to a good first
job whom they would then rule,

00:53:58.050 --> 00:54:00.130
of course, because
they'd be the CEOs

00:54:00.130 --> 00:54:01.920
where these other guys
would be the other.

00:54:01.920 --> 00:54:06.420
So there is a rumor that
in really good places which

00:54:06.420 --> 00:54:09.180
have really good
departments too, by the way,

00:54:09.180 --> 00:54:14.190
good students understand that
these are important subjects.

00:54:14.190 --> 00:54:17.970
And I know that to name
a non-Ivy prestige school

00:54:17.970 --> 00:54:20.220
but still a prestige
school, UT Austin,

00:54:20.220 --> 00:54:23.010
created some wonderful
humanities departments just

00:54:23.010 --> 00:54:28.380
by paying people at the Ivies
more than they got paid there

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:30.870
and luring them off and
creating wonderful French

00:54:30.870 --> 00:54:34.420
and classics and German
and history departments.

00:54:34.420 --> 00:54:37.560
You know, because it was--
because they were majors.

00:54:37.560 --> 00:54:40.920
I will say our administrators
don't think that.

00:54:40.920 --> 00:54:43.770
I was at a meeting where the
provost came in and said,

00:54:43.770 --> 00:54:46.881
of course I love the liberal
arts and I would support it.

00:54:46.881 --> 00:54:48.381
And I was silly
enough to say, well,

00:54:48.381 --> 00:54:51.960
you know, that's great, because
you could create the greatest

00:54:51.960 --> 00:54:55.230
classics or French or history
department in the world

00:54:55.230 --> 00:54:57.570
with half of what
it costs you to hire

00:54:57.570 --> 00:55:00.985
junior faculty in our
biology departments,

00:55:00.985 --> 00:55:03.640
in our physics departments, in
our chemistry departments where

00:55:03.640 --> 00:55:06.990
you need labs and all this
tremendous infrastructure.

00:55:06.990 --> 00:55:09.850
And of course, the
look on his face, he--

00:55:09.850 --> 00:55:13.100
tell when someone
isn't sincere, right?

00:55:13.100 --> 00:55:15.210
Well, yeah, well, we
could hire those people.

00:55:15.210 --> 00:55:17.940
But then when no students
took their courses, what would

00:55:17.940 --> 00:55:18.720
we do?

00:55:18.720 --> 00:55:20.630
We'd be stuck with them.

00:55:20.630 --> 00:55:22.110
So I said, OK.

00:55:22.110 --> 00:55:22.830
Thank you, sir.

00:55:22.830 --> 00:55:23.330
Thank you.

00:55:23.330 --> 00:55:24.720
I didn't understand.

00:55:24.720 --> 00:55:27.160
Thank you for clarifying that.

00:55:27.160 --> 00:55:30.600
So there is some evidence
that smart people know

00:55:30.600 --> 00:55:34.350
enough to study
the great subjects,

